The empirical analysis concentrates on FIBRATC14.MX. A multi-asset robustness check across the five equity tickers supplements the main results. All raw data are cached with MD5 checksums for reproducibility. A data validation layer checks for missing values, non-trading-day alignment, return calculation consistency, and extreme returns before any model is fitted.

% ============================================================
\section{Methodology}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Ex-Ante Regime Definition}

Volatility regimes are defined using information available only at the close of day $t-1$:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Realised volatility over the preceding 20 days:
    \begin{equation}
    \mathrm{rv}_{t-1} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{19} \sum_{k=1}^{20} (r_{t-k} - \bar{r}_{t-1})^{2}} \label{eq:rv}
    \end{equation}
    \item The 90\textsuperscript{th} percentile of $\mathrm{rv}$ over the preceding 500 days:
    \begin{equation}
    \Theta_{t-1} = Q_{0.90}\bigl( \mathrm{rv}_{t-500}, \dots, \mathrm{rv}_{t-1} \bigr) \label{eq:theta}
    \end{equation}
    \item If $\mathrm{rv}_{t-1} > \Theta_{t-1}$, day $t$ is labelled \hl{}; otherwise, \nor{}.
\end{enumerate}

The initial 520 days are used for regime construction. After intersecting with the rolling estimation window, the aligned evaluation period contains $n = 62$ observations for FIBRATC14.MX and $n = 95$ for FUNO11.MX.

\subsection{Models}

All models generate one-day-ahead VaR forecasts at the 95\% confidence level ($\alpha = 0.05$). Rolling estimation windows are $W = 125$ trading days, refitted every $R = 20$ days. The information set is identical across models and strictly no-look-ahead.

\paragraph{Historical VaR.}
The empirical $\alpha$-quantile of the preceding $W$ returns:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{VaR}_t^{\mathrm{Hist}}(\alpha) = -\,Q_{\alpha}\bigl(r_{t-W}, \dots, r_{t-1}\bigr) \label{eq:hist}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{GARCH(1,1)-$t$.}
\begin{align}
r_t &= \sigma_t \cdot \varepsilon_t, \qquad \varepsilon_t \sim t_{\nu} \text{ (standardised)} \nonumber\\
\sigma_t^{2} &= \omega + \alpha\, r_{t-1}^{2} + \beta\, \sigma_{t-1}^{2} \label{eq:garch}
\end{align}
Parameters, including the degrees of freedom $\nu$, are estimated by maximum likelihood \cite{bollerslev1986generalized,bollerslev1987conditionally}. VaR is $\mathrm{VaR}_t^{\mathrm{GARCH}}(\alpha) = -\hat{\sigma}_t \cdot F_{t_{\nu}}^{-1}(\alpha)$. A GARCH-Normal variant is also included for comparison.

\paragraph{XGBoost volatility models.}
Gradient boosting models predict conditional volatility $\sigma_{t+1}$ from lagged return and volatility features. VaR is derived parametrically as $\mathrm{VaR}_t = -\hat{\sigma}_{t} \cdot z_{\alpha}$ (Normal quantile). Two variants are estimated: XGBoost-Pure (29 base features) and XGBoost-Ensemble (augmented with GARCH-$t$ forecasts). Fixed parameters (100 trees, depth 3, learning rate 0.05) are identical across tickers.

\subsection{Backtesting Framework}

For model $m$, the breach indicator at date $t$ is $I_t^{(m)}(\alpha) = \mathbf{1}\{ r_t < -\mathrm{VaR}_t^{(m)}(\alpha) \}$. We employ four evaluation tools, ordered by reliability at small $n$:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Exact binomial test.} Under the null $H_0: p = \alpha$, the two-sided $p$-value is computed from the binomial distribution $\mathrm{Binomial}(n, \alpha)$ by summing the probability of all outcomes at least as extreme as the observed count $x = \sum I_t$. This test does not rely on asymptotic approximations.
    \item \textbf{Clopper--Pearson confidence interval.} An exact 95\% confidence interval for the true breach rate, derived from the relationship between the binomial and beta distributions: $\mathrm{CI} = [F^{-1}_{\beta}(\delta/2;\, x,\, n-x+1),\; F^{-1}_{\beta}(1-\delta/2;\, x+1,\, n-x)]$ where $\delta = 0.05$ and $F^{-1}_{\beta}$ denotes the beta quantile function.
    \item \textbf{Conservatism metric} $C = \hat{p} - \alpha$. A negative value indicates a conservative model (fewer breaches than expected); a positive value indicates an anti-conservative model (more breaches than expected).
    \item \textbf{Kupiec POF test \cite{kupiec1995techniques}.} Included for comparability with the existing literature, but its asymptotic $\chi^2_1$ null distribution is unreliable at $n < 100$. We report it as secondary context, not primary evidence.
\end{enumerate}

The Christoffersen independence test is computed but has negligible power at these sample sizes and is not emphasised.

\subsection{Power Analysis (Exact)}

We compute the rejection probability of the Kupiec test as a function of the true breach rate analytically, using the exact binomial distribution. For each possible breach count $x \in \{0, \dots, n\}$, we evaluate the Kupiec likelihood-ratio statistic and the exact binomial $p$-value, and determine whether each would reject at the 5\% level. The power at a given true breach probability $p_{\text{true}}$ is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Power}(p_{\text{true}}) = \sum_{x=0}^{n} \binom{n}{x} \, p_{\text{true}}^{x} \, (1-p_{\text{true}})^{n-x} \cdot \mathbf{1}\{\text{test rejects } H_0 \text{ at count } x\}.
\end{equation}
This approach is exact and, for $n \le 500$, runs in milliseconds.

% ============================================================
\section{Power Analysis and Size Distortion}
% ============================================================

Table~\ref{tab:power} reports the Type~I error rate (at $p_{\text{true}} = \alpha$) and power at selected alternative breach rates for the Kupiec and exact binomial tests at $n = 62$ and $n = 95$.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Exact operating characteristics of the Kupiec POF test and the exact binomial test at $\alpha = 0.05$. ``Size'' is the rejection rate when $p_{\text{true}} = \alpha$; ``Power'' is the rejection rate at the indicated true breach rate.}
\label{tab:power}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
$n$ & Test & Size (5\%) & Power (7\%) & Power (10\%) & Power (13\%) \\
\midrule
62  & Kupiec $\chi^2_1$ & 0.076 & 0.153 & 0.429 & 0.711 \\
62  & Exact binomial    & 0.035 & 0.142 & 0.427 & 0.711 \\
95  & Kupiec $\chi^2_1$ & 0.066 & 0.136 & 0.482 & 0.805 \\
95  & Exact binomial    & 0.028 & 0.129 & 0.482 & 0.805 \\
250 & Kupiec $\chi^2_1$ & 0.058 & 0.260 & 0.860 & 0.985 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Three patterns emerge. First, the Kupiec test's asymptotic $\chi^2$ approximation produces an inflated Type~I error rate: at $n = 62$, it rejects a correctly specified model 7.6\% of the time (nominal 5\%); at $n = 95$ the rate is 6.6\%. The exact binomial test is slightly conservative at very small $n$ (3.5\% rejection rate at $n = 62$) but converges to the nominal level as $n$ increases.

Second, power against economically meaningful misspecification is limited. A model that breaches 10\% of the time---double the nominal rate---is detected only 43\% of the time at $n = 62$ and 48\% at $n = 95$. Power does not exceed 80\% until the true breach rate reaches approximately 14\% at $n = 62$ or 13\% at $n = 95$.

Third, the difference between the Kupiec and exact binomial tests narrows for power computations (they agree to three decimal places at $p_{\text{true}} \ge 0.07$) but diverges at the null, where the exact test provides better size control.

Figure~\ref{fig:power} displays the full power curves. Figure~\ref{fig:size} shows the distribution of Kupiec $p$-values under the null, revealing the deviation from uniformity that drives the size distortion.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{../output/figures/power_curve_small_sample.pdf}
\caption{Power curves of the Kupiec POF test for $n = 62$, $n = 95$, and $n = 250$, computed exactly from the binomial distribution.}
\label{fig:power}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{../output/figures/size_distortion.pdf}
\caption{Distribution of Kupiec $p$-values under the null hypothesis ($H_0: p = 0.05$) for $n = 62$ and $n = 95$, based on 5,000 binomial draws. Deviation from the uniform distribution (dashed line) indicates size distortion. The empirical rejection rate (fraction below 0.05) is shown in each panel.}
\label{fig:size}
\end{figure}

The practical implication is straightforward: at $n \approx 60$--$95$, standard backtests cannot reliably distinguish between a well-calibrated model and one that is materially anti-conservative. A non-rejection does not imply good calibration; a rejection does not definitively identify a poor model. Additional information is necessary.

% ============================================================
\section{Empirical Results}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Main Analysis: FIBRATC14.MX ($n = 62$)}

Table~\ref{tab:fibraresults} reports the full set of backtesting metrics for the five models on the FIBRA Index ETF. The expected number of 95\% breaches under correct specification is $62 \times 0.05 = 3.10$, with a standard deviation of $1.72$.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{FIBRATC14.MX backtesting results ($n = 62$, 95\% VaR). $C = \hat{p} - \alpha$ is the conservatism metric; negative values indicate conservatism. The CP-CI is the 95\% Clopper--Pearson exact confidence interval for the breach rate.}
\label{tab:fibraresults}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Model & Breaches & Rate & $C$ & Exact $p$ & CP-CI \\
\midrule
GARCH-Normal     & 7 & 0.113 & +0.063 & 0.035 & [0.047, 0.219] \\
GARCH-$t$        & 3 & 0.048 & $-$0.002 & 1.000 & [0.010, 0.135] \\
XGBoost-Pure     & 7 & 0.113 & +0.063 & 0.035 & [0.047, 0.219] \\
XGBoost-Ensemble & 7 & 0.113 & +0.063 & 0.035 & [0.047, 0.219] \\
Historical VaR   & 4 & 0.065 & +0.015 & 0.553 & [0.018, 0.157] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

GARCH-$t$ is the model whose breach rate (4.8\%) is closest to the nominal 5\% level, with a conservatism metric of $C = -0.002$---approximately calibrated. GARCH-Normal is anti-conservative with 11.3\% breaches ($C = +0.063$), reflecting the thinner tails of the Normal distribution relative to the data. Both XGBoost-Pure and XGBoost-Ensemble produce identical breach rates of 11.3\% ($C = +0.063$), indicating that the GARCH-t volatility features added in the ensemble variant did not materially alter tail calibration. Historical VaR is mildly anti-conservative at 6.5\% ($C = +0.015$).

The exact binomial $p$-value is 0.035 for GARCH-Normal and both XGBoost variants. However, given the power analysis in Section~4, these rejections should be interpreted cautiously: at $n = 62$, the exact binomial test rejects a correctly specified model 3.5\% of the time, and the Kupiec test rejects it 7.6\% of the time. The evidence against these models is directionally meaningful but not statistically definitive in isolation.

The Christoffersen independence test yields $p \ge 0.10$ for all models at this sample size, providing no useful discrimination.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{../output/figures/pvalue_staircase.pdf}
\caption{Kupiec $p$-value as a function of the breach count $x$ for $n = 62$ and $\alpha = 0.05$. The diamond markers show the observed breach count of each model. At $x = 6$ the model is not rejected ($p = 0.132$); at $x = 7$ it is rejected ($p = 0.049$). Three models (GARCH-Normal, XGBoost-Pure, XGBoost-Ensemble) sit at $x = 7$, one breach past the 5\% threshold. A single additional breach---one trading day out of 62---flips the test outcome.}
\label{fig:staircase}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:staircase} makes the core statistical problem visible. The Kupiec $p$-value is a staircase function of the breach count, with large discontinuities at each integer step. At $n = 62$, moving from 6 to 7 breaches changes the $p$-value from 0.132 to 0.049---crossing the conventional 5\% threshold on the basis of a single observation. This is not a property of any model; it is an unavoidable consequence of the binomial distribution's discreteness at small $n$. The figure illustrates why an isolated $p$-value from a single ticker provides limited guidance.

The regime-specific breakdown is limited: the aligned set contains 38 Normal-regime and 7 High-Volatility-regime observations, plus 17 pre-regime dates. Sub-sample metrics on groups of 7 observations are not reported, as they would be unreliable.

\subsection{Multi-Asset Validation}

Table~\ref{tab:multi} extends the comparison to five tickers. The same fixed-specification models are used across all assets. Test windows range from $n = 62$ to $n = 96$.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Multi-asset comparison, 95\% VaR. $C = \hat{p} - \alpha$. All models use identical specifications across tickers.}
\label{tab:multi}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Ticker & $n$ & GARCH-$t$ rate & $C_t$ & XGBoost rate & $C_{\text{xgb}}$ \\
\midrule
FIBRATC14.MX & 62  & 0.048 & $-$0.002 & 0.113 & +0.063 \\
FUNO11.MX    & 95  & 0.021 & $-$0.029 & 0.084 & +0.034 \\
FIBRAPL14.MX & 95  & 0.042 & $-$0.008 & 0.137 & +0.087 \\
\^{}MXX      & 95  & 0.042 & $-$0.008 & 0.084 & +0.034 \\
